> Abstract
> Climate change poses significant fiscal challenges for governments, particularly in
> managing increasing damages and adaptation costs. This paper examines flood-
> ing and adaptation in the United Kingdom under two warming scenarios (global
> warming of 2◦C and 4◦C) for 2030 and 2050. Using a multi-sectoral CGE model,
> we analyse how different public financing mechanism affect fiscal sustainability and
> economic welfare. Financing mechanisms include (i) shifts in public expenditures
> maintaining a balanced budget, and (ii) debt financing via domestic green bonds
> or (iii) international green bonds. Without adaptation investments, we find GDP
> losses from destroyed capital and reduced productivity. Adaptation moderates these
> damages for all financing mechanisms, despite adaptation expenditures. However,
> ∗Corresponding author: Alexandra Lehner (alexandra.lehner@uni-graz.at).
> Acknowledgements: Part of this research was funded by the Horizon Europe project ACCREU (add
> project number).
> 1
> the net gain from adaptation depends on the warming scenario, time horizon and fi-
> nancing mechanism. In 2030, debt financing through international bonds is the most
> beneficial, maximizing public revenues while limiting crowding out of private invest-
> ment. However, by 2050, accumulated interest payments significantly impact the
> public budget, necessitating cuts in public consumption and leading to GDP losses
> compared to the other mechanisms. The results demonstrate that, while adaptation
> requires significant upfront investments, it improves long-term welfare, but with fis-
> cal outcomes depending on the chosen financing mechanism.
> Keywords: adaptation, government debt, green bonds, flooding, computable general
> equilibrium.
> JEL Codes: Q54; H54; H63; C68.
> 2
> 1 Introduction
> As reconfirmed by the 2024 United Nations Adaptation Gap Report, the gap between
> adaptation needs and implementation is increasing (UNEP, 2024). One reason for this
> gap is the substantial lack of adaptation finance, with adaptation finance so far coming
> predominantly from public sources (IPCC, 2023a). Apart from direct climate damages
> to capital and other assets, indirect economic damage costs may arise from system re-
> sponses and complex interconnections in the economic system, leading to price changes,
> income effects and GDP losses (Van Der Wijst et al., 2023). These damages are of
> high relevance to the governments and public finances, as tax income relies on economic
> activity (Jones et al., 2013). Moreover, climate-related extreme events necessitate pub-
> lic support for households and corporations through relief payments, reconstruction of
> destroyed state-owned assets, bail-outs of failing financial institutions, spending on pub-
> lic emergency measures and increased healthcare costs (Agarwala et al., 2021; Barrage,
> 2023). Decreased tax income and increased public spending requirements thus may strain
> governmental budgets (Bachner and Bednar-Friedl, 2019). Progressing climate change is
> projected to slow down economic growth and thereby further constrain fiscal space for
> adaptation(IPCC, 2023a).
> While mitigation can reduce future climate change impacts, adaptation measures aim to
> adjust to new climatic conditions to moderate risks (IPCC, 2023a). Planned adaptation
> is often seen as a responsibility of the state (Catalano et al., 2020; Woodruff et al., 2020),
> which however raises questions about fiscal sustainability, namely the balance between
> revenues and expenditures, the cost of debt, and the sustainability of public service de-
> livery and financial commitments (Ekins and Speck, 2013). However, planned adaptation
> involves upfront investments that primarily deliver benefits in the future. Therefore, while
> early adaptation measures with potentially longer-term benefits might strengthen the fis-
> cal resilience of public actors in the longer run (Gilmore et al., 2022), it raises the question
> about how to raise necessary funds in the short run.
> 3
> In general terms, the public sector can finance public adaptation through shifts in expen-
> diture allocations (e.g. from other spending areas to adaptation), increased taxes and/or
> through debt financing (Gilmore et al., 2022; Woodruff et al., 2020). These instruments
> haverepercussions, sothequestionofadaptationefficiencyandeffectiveness, aswellasthe
> justification for policy intervention are thus central to public investment decision making.
> These decisions are made more complex by the phasing of adaptation costs and benefits,
> as well as the high uncertainty around future climate change, which creates challenges
> for traditional economic appraisal and has led to a range of extended decision-support
> methods (Watkiss et al., 2015).
> The analysis of climate change risks, and adaptation responses, can use a wide variety
> of methodogical frameworks and specific questions. These include different approaches
> depending on the scale and aggregation level considered. Traditionally analysis was dom-
> inated by impact assessment methods and models, working at the sectoral level (for ex-
> ample, coastal or river flood risk and adaptation models (Dottori et al., 2023; Lincke and
> Hinkel, 2018)), and at an aggregate scale by global integrated assessment models that
> assessed the economic costs of climate change and indicative adaptation (Bruin et al.,
> 2009). This has widened in recent years (IPCC, 2023b) to include structural models
> (such as computable general equilibrium (CGE) modelling (Cortés Arbués et al., 2024)
> and econometric (statistical) studies (Kotz et al., 2024)).
> In contrast to the vast body of literature on macroeconomic effects of climate change im-
> pacts and adaptation, the topic of fiscal implications is still relatively scarce. Conceptual
> models provide valuable insights into the mechanisms how public budgets are affected
> both on the revenue and expenditure side (Ekins and Speck, 2014; Gilmore et al., 2022;
> Jones et al., 2013). Econometric modelling is useful for understanding how public budgets
> are currently affected by climate-related extremes (e.g. Beirne et al., 2021). The question
> of fiscal implications has also been linked to Integrated Assessment Modelling (Lamperti
> et al., 2019) and Agent-Based Modelling approaches (Dafermos et al., 2018; Gao, 2024;
> Zenios, 2022). A few CGE models provide insights into the indirect costs of impacts and
> 4
> adaptation (Bachner and Bednar-Friedl, 2019; Bachner et al., 2019, 2024; Parrado et al.,
> 2020).
> However, fiscal effects from different refinancing options for public adaptation are still
> under researched, and this is the research gap the current paper aims to address. In
> particular, three different financing options that are currently discussed in practice will
> be investigated: one demanding a balanced budget where public consumption is reduced
> accordingly; and two green bond options – one domestic (issued to private households),
> and one international (issued via financial markets). This will be applied to the case of
> adaptation to flooding in the United Kingdom (UK) for different warming scenarios and
> two different time frames (2030 and 2050).
> The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. Chapter 2 introduces the case
> study. Chapter 3 provides an overview on the CGE modeling framework, with a particular
> focus on the modeling of flood impacts, adaptation, and government budget implications.
> Chapter 4 presents the main results in terms of fiscal implications considering both direct
> andindirecteffectsviachangesinrevenues, aswellaseffectsonGDPandwelfare. Chapter
> 5 discusses the main findings, followed by a conclusion in Chapter 6.
> 2 Flood adaptation in the United Kingdom
> The United Kingdom has already experienced 1.2°C of warming from climate change
> and this is leading to increased shocks and stresses, from changes in the intensity and
> frequency of extreme events as well as from slow-onset climate change (CCC, 2021). The
> largest current impact, as one of the largest future risks, is associated with flooding, from
> the combination of river, surface water and coastal flooding (including from the effects of
> sea-level rise and storm surges).
> While the UK has always experienced flooding, and has generally high levels of flood
> protection, it still experiences high economic costs from extreme events (Environment
> 5
> Agency, 2018; NAO, 2023). There are an estimated 6.3 million residential properties
> (in England alone) in areas of medium and high flood risk (Environment Agency, 2025)
> and the current level of expected annual damage (EAD) from flooding are estimated at
> approximately £2 billion/year for the UK (Sayers et al., 2020). Future climate change will
> increasethesefloodrisksconsiderably. Forexample, withclimatechangethetotalnumber
> of properties in areas at risk from rivers and the sea or surface water (in England) could
> increase to around 8 million by mid-century (EA, 2025), with strong increase in projected
> EAD.
> There is a statutory requirement – as set out in the Climate Change Act of 2008 (UK
> Government,2008)-fortheGovernmenttoundertakeanassessmentoftheclimaterisksto
> theUKeveryfiveyears. TheUKhasnowcompletedthreeroundsofthispolicycycle, with
> the latest assessment being the 3rd Climate Change Risk Assessment (CCRA3), published
> in 2021 (CCC, 2021; HMG, 2022). There is also a duty for Government to publish a
> national adaptation programme after the CCRA, setting out how the Government will
> address these risks. In the UK, adaptation is devolved, and so the countries of England,
> Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland prepare their own adaptation programmes. In
> England, the3rdNationalAdaptationProgrammewaspublishedin2023(DEFRA,2024).
> This sets out the English Government’s proposed action over the next 5 years (2023-2028).
> A major pillar of successive NAP programmes has been to increase investment in flood
> protection. This has led to a sharp rise in flood investment over the last decade. Most
> recently, the Government announced a £5.2 billion flood and coastal defences protection
> capital funding programme for 2021-2027 for England (DEFRA, 2021). There has also
> been a detailed future capital investment programme set out, to look at future investment
> needs to address rising climate change risks, published in the Long-Term Investment
> Scenarios (LTIS) (Environment Agency, 2019) for England. This identified that the long-
> term optimal level of investment (depending on policy choices) could range from £1.0
> billion to £1.2 billion (in 2019 prices) for England over coming decades, but that much
> 6
> higher investment costs would be needed in a more extreme warming scenario. Similar
> investment programmes are also taking place in the other devolved administrations.
> The investment for flood protection spending comes almost entirely from the government
> budget and is significant (approximately 1%) when compared to total public spending,
> which totals just under £1.2 trillion (HMT, 2024a). The projected increase in capital
> spending to address climate change therefore will put pressure on the budget, and climate
> change (including flood protection) is now recognised as a major risk to the fiscal sustain-
> ability of the UK, as evidence in the latest report by the indpendent economic watchdog
> in their assessment of fiscal risks and sustainability (OBR, 2024).
> Without plannedincreases in adaptation(floodprotection), climate change is projected to
> lead to higher damages to residental and non-residential properties, as well as the govern-
> ment estate, and to infrastructure (public and private), increasing repair and maintance.
> There are also indirect effects to the economy, from the impacts on transport, business
> disruption. Flooding is also associated with health impacts, including deaths and injuries,
> but also mental health impacts (following flooding), leading to reductions in productivity
> and additional treatment costs (Environment Agency, 2021; Waite et al., 2017). All of
> this means that future flood risks from climate change will increase public spending and
> reduce government income.
> Adaptation (increased flood protection) can reduce these future costs, but it requires in-
> creased investment. It is therefore critical to explore whether adaptation has net benefical
> economic (societal) benefits, as well as positive fiscal benefits. The results of such analysis
> influence the level of adaptation investment, but also the choice of how this additional
> spending is financed. The latter involves choices over whether increased flood invest-
> ment should arise from a reallocation of the current public spending budget, or financed
> through borrowing (debt) and if the latter, how different potential financing models might
> influence the net impacts on the public finances.
> For this analysis, data has been compiled for the current and expected expected annual
> damage for the UK, from the combined effects of river, surface and coastal flooding from
> 7
> published CCRA3 sources, with and without adaptation (Sayers et al., 2020; Watkiss,
> Paul et al., 2021) and combined with data on current capital and recuring costs for flood
> protection (DEFRA, 2021; Environment Agency, 2021) complemented with estimates
> for other DAs. The analysis of future risks considers the the future years 2030 and
> 2050, planning for two different warming scenarios as used in CCRA3, associated with
> 2 degrees and 4 degrees of global average warming by the end of the century relative
> to pre-industrial. Expected annual damages from fluvial, surface and coastal flooding
> were broken down into by residential (private households), non-residential (commerical
> buildings), tangible (public infrastructure) and intangible damages (health impacts), as
> shown in figure 1. These results were then used as an input for the CGE modelling.
