> 3 Methodology
> The CGE model used is based on the COIN-INT model, first introduced by Schinko et al.
> (2014) for carbon leakage assessment and further developed in Knittel et al. (2020), Bach-
> ner et al. (2022) and Preinfalk et al. (2024) for climate change impact and adaptation
> 8
> assessments. It is implemented in GAMS using the MPSGE framework1 and is a global,
> multi-regional, multi-sectoral computable general equilibrium (CGE) model. In the fol-
> lowing, the static version of the model is applied to the case study of the UK and analyses
> consequences from future impacts and adaptation measures under two warming scenarios,
> as described above.
> 3.1 Model description
> In the COIN-INT model, each regional economy consists of a productive economy with
> free markets, consuming households that are endowed with production factors, and within
> Europe, a public household collecting taxes used for different public expenditures. The
> model follows a neoclassical general equilibrium model, where all markets clear by ad-
> justing relative prices (while the welfare measure of the region of North America being
> the numeraire) under full employment of a fixed supply of production factors (Robinson,
> 2006).
> The model is calibrated using the Global Trade Analysis Project (GTAP9) database for
> the reference year 2011. This Data Base is a macroeconomic representation of the world
> economy and includes bilateral trade, transport and social accounting matrices (SAM) for
> 140 world regions (Aguiar et al., 2016). The present data was aggregated and adapted to
> the model’s needs, represented diagrammatically in figure 2.
> In the COIN-INT model, the world economy is aggregated to 26 geographical regions and
> 20 economic sectors. The resolution is higher for the European continent, consisting of 21
> regions, and more coarse elsewhere, represented by 5 additional regions.Each region dis-
> poses of a domestic production consisting of representative firms in the form of economic
> sectors. These sectors include four agricultural sectors, five extraction and electricity sec-
> tors, two manufacturing industries, a machinery/electronic equipment and motor sector,
> three other industries, one transport sector, three service sectors and a construction sec-
> tor. Thus, these 20 individual sectors represent primary, secondary and tertiary sectors in
