> Final demand in EU countries is modeled via two representative households, one private
> and one public household. In the rest of the world, there is no public household, only
> 10
> a private household. Households use their income to consume goods and services. Con-
> sumption of goods and services is implemented as a nested CES function, which generates
> welfare, maximisation of which is the objective of the households.
> The representative private household is endowed with production factors, which employ-
> ment pays the households income. Additionally to this income, the household may receive
> social transfers from the government. The generated income is then split between con-
> sumption and savings. Savings are flexible and are fully invested, resulting in a savings-
> investment balance and a model closure.
> The public household in EU countries represents the government, which collects taxes,
> executes public services (denoted as public expenditures or public consumption), and
> distributestransfers. Socialtransferstohouseholdsareassumedtobefixedtothebaseline,
> meaning that they are independent of changes to public income. This assumption is later
> lifted in the sensitivity analysis. Public tax income determines public expenditures in a
> balanced budget assumption, while tax rates are fixed, leading to potentially fluctuating
> tax income depending on changes in the tax base. The model is subject to a government
> budget closure, not allowing for additional net state-deficit in the baseline. In adaptation
> scenarios however, adaptation investments in the UK may be financed by public deficit
> through green bond issuing. Still, there is no endogenous debt decision, meaning that
> any additional debt is implemented as an exogenous political choice and the government
> balance closure still holds.
> 3.2 Implementation of flooding impacts and adaptation
> Flood impacts materialise in the model through different channels, specified in table 1.
> Residential and non-residential damages are implemented as a negative capital shock to
> thehouseholdthatisendowedwiththeproductionfactor,asthesedamagesareinterpreted
> as capital destruction. Tangible damages are implemented as reduced capital productivity
> in the public services sector (OSG). When public infrastructure is damaged, this reduces
> 11
> its productivity and requires increased maintenance. Intangible damages in the form
> of increased public health costs are implemented as a shift in the public consumption
> towards the public services sector (OSG) while proportionally decreasing consumption of
> non-health goods and services.
> Adaptation on the one hand decreases the impacts of climate change, but on the other
> imposes additional investment costs to the public household. The adaptation costs are
> fixed exogenously and implemented as "forced" investments, while being financed in three
> different ways: By keeping the budget balanced and cutting public consumption expen-
> ditures, or by deficit spending by issuing governmental green bonds either to domestic or
> foreign households.
> One central assumption is that adaptation is defined as public planned adaptation, while
> system responses to flood impacts are understood as part of the impact costs. Moreover,
> public adaptation is treated as an exogenous policy decision and not as an endogenous
> choice made by the governmental actor in the model.
> The UK government is already undertaking such activity through a green finance pro-
> gramme, which includes adaptation (HMT, 2024b). This is using two different modalities.
> First, the sale of National Savings and Investments’ (NS&I) retail Green Savings Bonds.
> This is targeting domestic households (NS&I, 2025 ). The second is the issuance of green
> gilts (green bonds) via the Debt Management Office (DMO) (DMO, 2025 ). This is tar-
> geting the financial markets. While this is targeting domestic and international investors,
> for the modelling, an analysis is included where debt if financed by international (foreign)
> households.
