> 3.4 Scenario framework
> The model is forward-looking, based on assumptions about future developments. The
> model takes the year 2011 as a benchmark starting point. Two static baseline time
> horizons are considered - near future (2030) and far future (2050). Future baseline sce-
> narios in the models, which are representative futures without climate change impacts or
> adaptation policies, are calibrated using SSP2-RCP4.5 as a scenario combination for all
> regions (Moss et al., 2010; O’Neill et al., 2014; Van Vuuren et al., 2011).This means that
> population developments, economic growth trajectories, and carbon taxes in the baseline
> scenario are based on a moderate, middle-of-the-road development in the socio-economic
> space, while no climate change impacts are present.
> ImpactandImpact+Adaptationscenariosareimplementedascounterfactualstothebase-
> line scenario in 2030 and 2050. The impact scenarios include annual flood impacts with
> the different forms of damages to capital, productivity, and increased public expendi-
> tures. The model then adjusts and results in a new equilibrium which is different from
> the baseline scenario, where no climate impacts are assumed. The impact and adaptation
> scenario models the reduced impacts with adaptation, and thus the benefit of adaptation,
> but there is an additional cost, again leading to a new equilibrium that can be compared
> to the baseline and to the impact scenario.
> For the implementation of the UK-specific impact- and impact/adaptation-scenarios,
> there are two climate change scenarios considered, which result in 2 degree and 4 de-
> gree warming respectively until the end of the century. This framework is consistent with
> the CCRA3.
