> 4.1 Public budget effects
> Results for the fiscal space are depicted in figure 4, split into income and expenditure
> differences compared to the baseline. Since public tax income depends on economic activ-
> ity, climate impacts lead to decreased public income, forcing the government to decrease
> expenditures by the same amount (2030: -0.17% / 2050: -0.28% compared to the baseline
> scenario). As transfers are kept fixed in our analysis, all losses accrue to the public con-
> 15
> sumption side (-0.18% / -0.30%). Without further specification on which governmental
> consumption expenditures are cut, all public services are affected in equal proportions.
> These cuts also includes crucial services such as health care, education and public security.
> For all adaptation financing options, total public income and expenditures are higher in
> the scenario with adaptation investments compared to in the impact scenario without
> adaptation. In the case of balanced budgets, transfers are kept fixed and public adapta-
> tion is financed by decreasing general governmental expenditures for public consumption
> (-0.35% compared to the baseline scenario, decrease by 0.18 percentage points (%pt)
> compared to the impact scenario / -0.38%, decrease by 0.08%pt). Resulting tax income
> gains (compared to the impact scenario) enable higher total public expenditures (-0.14%,
> increase by 0.03 %pt / -0.21%, increase by 0.07%pt). This means that—although adapta-
> tion is financed through expenditure cuts in public consumption—the overall fiscal space
> can be improved when compared to the impact scenario.
> In the domestic green bond financing case, governmental green bonds are issued instead
> of cutting expenditures. This results in a deficit-to-GDP ratio of 0.04% in 2030 and
> 0.03% in 2050. Positive effects on tax income are evident (-0.14%, increase by 0.03%pt /
> -0.19%, increase by 0.09%). However, these positive public income effects do not translate
> to higher public consumption expenditures, which are slightly lower than in the impact
> scenario (-0.19%, decrease by 0.01%pt / -0.34%, decrease by 0.03%pt). This is due to the
> fact that interest has to be paid to bondholders, crowding out public consumption. In
> 2030, the share of interest payments amounts to 0.04% of total public expenditures, and
> substantially increase to 0.13% in 2050. Total public expenditures are higher in 2030 and
> 2050, with those in 2030 even exceeding the expenditures in the baseline scenario without
> climate change impacts (+0.05%, increase by 0.22%pt / -0.03%, increase by 0.25%pt).
