> This study applies the CGE model COIN-INT to the case study of indirect economic ef-
> fects from flood impacts and public planned adaptation in the UK. It focuses on macroe-
> conomic and fiscal effects in the near future (2030) and far future (2050) for two warming
> scenarios. This study explored macroeconomic and fiscal implications of future flood
> impacts and different financing options for adaptation measures. The findings help to
> understand short-term and long-term considerations of different financing options for the
> public and highlight the importance of indirect effects on private actors in the economy.
> We find that public planned adaptation is able to alleviate a range of flood losses in
> the future, which are expected to increase with evolving climate change, but the choice of
> financinghaseffectsonboththefiscalspaceandthemacroeconomyasawhole. Bycutting
> governmental consumption in favour of adaptation measures, the budget is kept balanced,
