> term significant seepage of interest payments to abroad has to be taken into consideration.
> Additionally, bond financing may have effects on future lending conditions. Still, deficit
> financing of adaptation investments can be justified by its effectiveness and may help to
> 24
> construct a stock of critical green infrastructure that pays off over time. In all cases, GDP
> and private welfare losses can be alleviated, especially in the long run.
> The study aimed to gain a more thorough understanding of fiscal implications of different
> adaptation financing options, while limitations have to be considered. In our assessment,
> we assumed that changes to the fiscal space would affect all types of public consumption
> expenditures in the same manner, which is usually not the case. In practice, certain
> departmental budgets are ring-fenced, which might mean even more severe cuts in partic-
> ular areas. Moreover, distributional effects are not considered in our analysis, as private
> households are modelled as one representative actor. It is reasonable to assume that pub-
> lic consumption expenditure cuts as well as residual flood impacts will hit lower income
> households in a more pronounced manner. Moreover, domestic bond issuance will benefit
> higher income households more. Furthermore, we do not model implicit interest rates
> or endogenous financial decisions. Effects on lending conditions and ratings or further
> financial market implications are not shown due to the real economy nature of the model.
> The choice of model closings and parameters may also impact outcomes.
> In conclusion, we showed that public planned adaptation to the increasing flood risk has
> a high societal return in terms of GDP, but the choice of financing option has important
> implications for the fiscal space and public acceptability. Future research may expand on
> more financing options, such as the introduction of new taxes, but also more advanced
> financing strategies, such as mandatory adaptation funds or other forms of leveraging
