> 
> Flooding poses a substantial climate-change-associated risk in the UK, already evident with 1.2°C of warming and expected to increase significantly under future warming scenarios. The largest future risks from climate change are associated with combined river, surface water, and coastal flooding, exacerbated by sea-level rise and storm surges (CCC, 2021). Currently, approximately 6.3 million properties in England are in areas of medium to high flood risk, incurring an estimated £2 billion per year in expected annual damages (Environment Agency, 2021). Without adaptation, projected climate change could increase the number of at-risk properties to 8 million by mid-century, leading to significant economic impacts, including higher damages to residential, non-residential, and state-owned properties, infrastructure damage, and broader economic disruptions (Sayers et al., 2020; HMG, 2022). The UK government's assessments, as outlined in the Climate Change Act of 2008 and subsequent National Adaptation Programmes, underline the critical importance of flood protection investments, which have already increased significantly in recent years to mitigate these risks (DEFRA, 2024; HMT, 2024).
> 
